a new report by USA Today raises  concerns about mental health risks  linked to high potency marijuana  products and the potential for psychotic  episodes Jamie yuccas is in our Los  Angeles Bureau Jamie what evidence did  USA Today look at well Anthony the  papers spoke with medical professionals  and a dozen parents whose children  suffered mental breakdowns it focused on  high potency THC products like vapes and  oils and some children died by suicide  or had suicidal thoughts I woke up in a  mental hospital I never thought I was  going to be there ever  Madison McIntosh says less than a year  ago he started vaping high potency THC  after his lifelong dream to play  professional baseball hit a dead end he  says he used THC to get him out of his  depression in reality was actually just  putting me even more so in the hole of  depression and unhappiness the 24 year  old says it got to the point where he  was vaping multiple times a day up until  March that's when his boss called his  dad and stepmom to say Madison was  acting strangely I started to talk with  him and within 30 seconds I knew  something was wrong like he was out of  his mind  Madison's older brother took this cell  phone video that night  what is the noise my brain  at the hospital Madison's parents say  doctors diagnosed him with cannabis  induced psychosis I grew up in the 70s  early 80s you never heard of cannabis  induced psychosis what is that I looked  at a kid who had everything going for  him and I had to stare at him sitting on  a hospital bed with a sheet and a pillow  in this room stark white jane O'Donnell  co-authored the USA Today article titled  in part is marijuana linked to psychosis  in the 1980's the marijuana that anybody  that smoked marijuana was using often  had far less than even 10 percent THC  content it is very common for people to  be smoking something with 90% THC  content now the article cites one study  that found a 77 percent increase in  suicide deaths in Colorado among 10 to  19 year olds with marijuana in their  systems is not clear and may never be  clear whether cannabis caused a person  to have psycho affective disorders there  certainly is a link between the heavy  cannabis use and the schizophrenia or  the psychosis and it certainly made  worse UCLA professor Timothy Fong is an  addiction specialist who studies  cannabis he says while more research is  needed in some severe cases cannabis can  be linked to psychosis but for the vast  majority of men and women who use  cannabis in a adult fashion in a  reasonable manner and who obtain  cannabis from a regulated and legal  dispensary those risks are fairly low  now we reached out to several marijuana  advocacy groups for their response to  the article the group called normal told  us cannabis should not be viewed as a  harmless substance it says because it  may pose certain health risks to  potentially vulnerable populations it  should be regulated to keep it away from  those who may be at higher risk for  experiencing an adverse reaction  Anthony all right Jimmy thanks
 

